---
const phrases = [
  ["the curious fox", "Names who is involved: a noun phrase."],
  ["beside the gate", "Tells where: a prepositional phrase."],
  ["after the storm", "Tells when."]
];
---

<div class="topic-sheet">
  <h1>Sentence Structure - Sentences and phrases</h1>
  <p>
    A <strong>sentence</strong> expresses a complete thought. It normally has a <strong>subject</strong> (who
    or what it is about) and a <strong>verb</strong> (what happens, or what the subject is). It begins with a
    capital letter and ends with suitable punctuation.
  </p>
  <section class="summary-block summary-sand">
    <h2>Subject and predicate</h2>
    <p>
      <strong>The curious fox</strong> <strong>waited</strong> beside the gate.
    </p>
    <p>Subject: <strong>the curious fox</strong>. Predicate: <strong>waited beside the gate</strong>.</p>
    <p>Ask “Who or what?” then “What about it?” In <strong>Wait here!</strong> the subject <em>you</em> is understood.</p>
  </section>
  <section class="summary-block summary-blue">
    <h2>A phrase is not a full statement</h2>
    <p>
      A <strong>phrase</strong> works as a unit, but it does not have its own subject and complete verb. A
      noun phrase can be as short as <strong>fox</strong>. Test what the words do in the sentence.
    </p>
    <table class="punct-table summary-table">
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th class="th-feature" scope="col">Phrase</th>
          <th class="th-rule" scope="col">What it contributes</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        {
          phrases.map(([phrase, job]) => (
            <tr>
              <th scope="row">{phrase}</th>
              <td>{job}</td>
            </tr>
          ))
        }
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </section>
</div>
